  "name": "Canzone romanesca (Roman dialect popular song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Popular song in Romanesco, the dialect of Rome, which grew out of the stornelli and serenades of the city's working districts and found a public stage in the San Giovanni song festival of the late nineteenth century and in café-chantant and variety theatre, where Ettore Petrolini made his name. After the Second World War singers such as Gabriella Ferri, Lando Fiorini and Alvaro Amici recorded it and sang it in Trastevere nightspots and on television, and songs from the stage musical Rugantino joined the repertoire.",
  "recipe": "classic European mid-1960s canzone romanesca, ballad Poetry, Mediterranean ballad branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment, single lead single track no stacking. classical nylon-string guitar, bowl back Italian Italian alpine spruce brescian calace mandolin. live tracking. Neumann U67, tube pre, colored vintage console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips echo plate reverb.",
